{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c56f","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a07916-2a5a-7c91-941e-599b9b859136"},"sequence":274,"recorded_at":1780531400000543,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"256d89a1-396a-4382-a1d7-c6813bb31cc7","payload_type":"task.lifecycle.output","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"task_lifecycle","command_id":"256d89a1-396a-4382-a1d7-c6813bb31cc7","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"256d89a1-396a-4382-a1d7-c6813bb31cc7"},"task_stream":{"kind":"task","id":"e2d100d0-285b-4854-85b2-6157b16afe9b"},"task_id":"e2d100d0-285b-4854-85b2-6157b16afe9b","event":{"kind":"output","task_id":"e2d100d0-285b-4854-85b2-6157b16afe9b","chunk":"Read text file `.temp/TASK-260906-vmzk0y/verdict.md`.\n1|# TASK-260906-vmzk0y — review verdict, CR revision 2\n2|\n3|Verdict: **changes requested** (one blocking finding, two notes).\n4|repeat-of: `rev1/F3` — class only: *a claim in shipped text asserted from a\n5|proxy signal rather than measured, which measurement contradicts*. It is a new\n6|instance at a new site (`provhost.parseMajor`), introduced by the round-2 F2\n7|fix. rev1/F3 itself is measurably closed (see G-D). rev1/F4 shares the\n8|sub-shape \"the code's own doc says otherwise\".\n9|\n10|Candidate tree `be5b2844b8996b4764e1cd7a0dfccf1facf478d2` — verified equal to\n11|the worktree by a detached-index `read-tree HEAD` + `add -A` + `write-tree`,\n12|and re-verified byte-identical after every mutant below (`TREE INTACT`).\n13|`ls-tree` shows the four new files (`provider/lift.go`, `provider/lift_test.go`,\n14|`terminalbackend/descriptor.go`, `terminalbackend/descriptor_test.go`) are\n15|in-tree. 15 changed paths, matching the record.\n16|\n17|## Verdict summary\n18|\n19|Round 2 closes F1, F2, F4, F5 measurably and rewrites F3's bound around facts\n20|that reproduce. 23 reviewer mutants applied, 23 killed, 0 survivors. The one\n21|blocking finding is a **new** defect introduced by the F2 fix itself: the\n22|saturating `return` in `parseMajor` short-circuits the minor/patch validation,\n23|so a malformed version string is now reported as a *recognized* protocol major.\n24|No admission results — but the shipped classification contradicts two doc\n25|comments in this same diff, bypasses the v2 member-vocabulary gate for that\n26|class, and **nothing in the suite pins it**: the corrected behaviour passes the\n27|whole provhost suite unchanged.\n28|\n29|## What I ran myself (nothing accepted from the outcome document)\n30|\n31|| Gate | Result |\n32|| --- | --- |\n33|| `go build ./...` | exit 0 |\n34|| `go vet ./...` | exit 0 |\n35|| `go test ./...` | exit 0, no FAIL |\n36|| `go test -cover` provider / provhost / terminalbackend | 97.8% / 85.8% / 94.2% — reproduces the outcome exactly |\n37|| `gofmt -l internal/` | clean |\n38|| `go run ./internal/traceability/cmd/tracecheck` | exit 0, contracts=60 acceptance_cases=98 — reproduces |\n39|| provhost refusal-arm bijection | 167/167 derived arms witnessed |\n40|| terminalbackend declared rows | 209 table rows + 1 funnel row = **210**, bijection green — the outcome's \"210/210\" is exact |\n41|| candidate tree OID after all mutants | `be5b2844…` unchanged |\n42|\n43|## G-A (blocking gate) — overflow class: **closed**\n44|\n45|Census of every digit→bounded-number site **in this leaf** (3 sites; the\n46|`strconv`-based sites elsewhere in the repo are out of leaf and untouched):\n47|\n48|| Site | Shape | Refuses before accumulating? |\n49|| --- | --- | --- |\n50|| `terminalbackend/descriptor.go:243` `descriptorGeometry` | pre-check `value > 100` inside the loop, before the multiply | yes — accumulator is capped at 1009, cannot wrap on any platform |\n51|| `terminalbackend/terminalbackend.go:259` `semverMajor` | `major > (MaxInt-digit)/10` → saturate | yes |\n52|| `provhost/protocol.go:379` `parseMajor` | `major > (MaxInt-step)/10` → saturate | yes (but see G1) |\n53|\n54|Driven through the real production entry points, **with the control first**:\n55|\n56|| Vector | `AdmitProviderDescriptor` result |\n57|| --- | --- |\n58|| `columns = 80` (control) | ADMITTED as 80 |\n59|| `columns = 1000` / `rows = 1000` (boundary) | ADMITTED as 1000 |\n60|| `columns = 1001` / `rows = 1001` | REFUSED `descriptor geometry bound` |\n61|| `0`, `-1`, `80.0`, `8e1` | REFUSED (`bound` / `digits` arms, correctly split) |\n62|| `0080` | REFUSED at `document syntax` (strict decoder) |\n63|| `2^63`, `2^64+1`, `2^64+500`, `2^64+1000` | REFUSED `descriptor geometry bound` |\n64|| `55340232221128655348` (double overflow → 500) | REFUSED same arm |\n65|| 100 nines, `1000000000000000000000000000000080` | REFUSED same arm |\n66|\n67|Both members (`columns` *and* `rows`) driven separately for every vector, so the\n68|two call sites of the shared helper are measured independently.\n69|\n70|`semverMajor` through every production entry that consumes it, with the refusal\n71|naming the major arm and not a downstream membership/ordering bound:\n72|\n73|| Entry | `18446744073709551617.0.0` (wraps to 1) | `36893488147419103233.0.0` (double overflow → 1) |\n74|| --- | --- | --- |\n75|| `ParseProviderDescriptor` | REFUSED `descriptor protocol version` | REFUSED same |\n76|| `New(…)` | REFUSED `protocol_versions major 1` | REFUSED same |\n77|| `CheckVersionTuple(…)` | REFUSED `protocol_version major 1` | REFUSED same |\n78|\n79|**Accumulation planted back at each site** (the fix is not a bypassable\n80|pre-check):\n81|\n82|| Mutant | Class | Verdict | Killing test |\n83|| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n84|| RV1 geometry pre-check deleted | arm-deletion | KILLED | `TestParseProviderDescriptorGeometryOverflowVectors` — `want refusal \"descriptor geometry bound\", got admission` |\n85|| RV2 final bound `1000`→`1001` | narrowing | KILLED | `…GeometryOverflowVectors` + `ValueRefusals/columns_over_bound` |\n86|| RV3 lower bound `1`→`0` | narrowing | KILLED | `ValueRefusals/columns_zero`, `/rows_zero` |\n87|| RV5 `semverMajor` guard neutered to `major > math.MaxInt` (never fires ⇒ accumulation restored) | narrowing | KILLED | `TestParseProviderDescriptorProtocolVersionOverflowVectors` |\n88|| RV10 `parseMajor` guard neutered the same way | narrowing | KILLED | `TestParseMajorNeverWraps` — `parseMajor(\"18446744073709551617.0.0\") = 1` |\n89|| RV4 `semverMajor` saturation deleted outright | — | COMPILE_FAIL (unused `math`) — control row, **not** a kill; re-anchored as RV5 |\n90|\n91|Note on mutant class for `descriptorGeometry`: narrowing the *pre-check*\n92|constant upward cannot create a hole (with threshold T the accumulator peaks at\n93|`T*10+9`, so a wrap needs T ≈ MaxInt/10, i.e. deletion). Arm-deletion is the\n94|correct class for that guard; the narrowing obligation is discharged by RV2/RV3\n95|against the *final* bound, which is a separate rule. Both arms carry the same\n96|`Detail`, and the inventory declares them as occurrences #1 and #2 — RV7\n97|confirms the bijection notices when either disappears.\n98|\n99|## G-B (blocking gate) — F4's registered-but-unlifted case: **closed**\n100|\n101|Which answer was chosen: **fail closed** — `Lift` returns `(nil, failure)` and\n102|mints no wire object. Independently confirmed that `not_found` really is\n103|registered (so the round-1 hole was reachable) and that every registered code\n104|outside the three lift arms takes the same closed path:\n105|\n106|| `Lift(Error{code: …})` | registry | result |\n107|| --- | --- | --- |\n108|| `invalid_config` | registered, exit 3 | WIRE, static message, exit 3 |\n109|| `local_precondition_failed` | registered, exit 3 | WIRE, static message, exit 3 |\n110|| `integrity_failure` | registered, exit 9 | WIRE, static message, exit 9 |\n111|| **`not_found`** | **registered, exit 4** | **REFUSED, wire object nil, NOT the unregistered arm, input handed back** |\n112|| `capability_unavailable` | registered, exit 6 | REFUSED, same closed sub-arm |\n113|| `incompatible_protocol` | registered, exit 6 | REFUSED, same closed sub-arm |\n114|| `timeout`, `internal_error`, `\"\"`, `bogus_code` | unregistered | REFUSED with `ErrUnregisteredCode` |\n115|\n116|Mutants:\n117|\n118|| Mutant | Class | Verdict | Killing test |\n119|| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n120|| RV13 default arm fails open again (registered code mints a generic-message wire object) | narrowing | KILLED | `TestLiftRefusesRegisteredCodeWithoutLiftArm` |\n121|| RV14 registry check deleted from the default arm | arm-deletion | KILLED | `TestLiftRefusesUnregisteredCode` |\n122|| RV15 lift appends the local detail to the wire message | narrowing | KILLED | `TestLiftCarriesDiscoveryFailuresToTheWire` — `lifted message = \"…: cannot list plugin_dirs[0] directory \\\"/plugins\\\"\"` |\n123|\n124|The two sub-arms are pinned by two different named tests with different\n125|`errors.Is` assertions, so neither can pass on the other's behaviour. The\n126|causal-leak negative drives the real `axerror.New` gate and asserts\n127|`ErrCausalLeak` by name, with the control (the secret is present in the local\n128|rendering) asserted first in both directions.\n129|\n130|## G-C (blocking gate) — F5's derivation and the four required plants: **closed**\n131|\n132|All four plants present and each one is real — I neutered the corresponding\n133|production behaviour and watched the named plant die:\n134|\n135|| Mutant | Class | Verdict | Killing test |\n136|| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n137|| RV16 census drops `token.VAR` (the `code` token preserved) | census-only, token-preserving | KILLED | `TestDerivedCodesIncludesVarBinding` — `derived codes = map[held_code:true], want \"planted_var_code\"` |\n138|| RV17 census reads only `provider.go` | census-only, token-preserving | KILLED | `TestDerivedCodesScansEveryPackageFile` (+ two others) |\n139|| RV19 code-prefixed **type alias** no longer fails closed | census-only | KILLED | `TestDerivedCodesFailsClosedOnUnclassifiableBinding` |\n140|| RV18b non-literal value `continue`s instead of erroring | census-only | KILLED | `…FailsClosedOnUnclassifiableBinding` — `identifier-valued derivation = map[base_code:true]` |\n141|\n142|Grouped consts are covered by `TestDerivedCodesIncludesGroupedConsts`; the\n143|inherited-empty-value-list and shared-value-list shapes fail closed by\n144|construction. The derivation is unclassifiable-fails-closed, not skip.\n145|Denominator is production-derived (3 codes) and `TestLiftCoversTheClosedCodeSet`\n146|runs it in both directions (unwitnessed code ⇄ orphan vector).\n147|\n148|## G-D (blocking gate) — F3's bound: **rests on reproducible facts**\n149|\n150|Every sub-claim of the rewritten bound checked against source, not against the\n151|outcome:\n152|\n153|| Claim in `identity.go` | Verified |\n154|| --- | --- |\n155|| `VerifyObjectIdentity` on the §5.5 example returns the example's own `record_id`, nil error | yes — `sha256:c879d766…e220c2` is literally the example's `record_id`; the test asserts field *and* digest, not nil-error-only |\n156|| `VerifyObjectIdentity` has **zero** production call sites repo-wide | yes — independent grep: only the definition (`canonicaljson/canonical.go:309`) and two comments |\n157|| `CheckIdentity` requires `record_id` to be a well-formed digest but never recomputes it | yes — `identity.go:169`, `isDigest` only |\n158|| the conjoined `CalculateObjectIdentity` entry discards its digest | yes — `identity.go:288`, `_, _, err :=` |\n159|| the three terminal schemas *do* recompute the omit-self digest | yes — `terminalbackend/manifest.go:682` `checkIdentity` |\n160|| the old \"merely illustrative\" justification is withdrawn *in the bound text* | yes — stated explicitly, not paraphrased away |\n161|\n162|**Control plant:** I added a production call to `VerifyObjectIdentity` in\n163|`internal/provhost/` and `TestNoProductionPathAttestsProviderIdentityBinding`\n164|reddened naming the site; removed, it passes and logs `125 production files, 2\n165|parsed, 0 production call sites` — and 125 is exactly `find internal cmd -name\n166|'*.go' -not -name '*_test.go' | wc -l`. The scan is not vacuous.\n167|\n168|## G-E — F6 / F7 notes: **corrected**\n169|\n170|- **F6** — `AdmitProviderDescriptor` still has zero production callers, and\n171|  `descriptor.go:20-31` says so in the code, naming the future call path, the\n172|  required order (parse, match, then launch) and the two miswirings that would\n173|  admit a stale binding. Independently confirmed: no non-comment caller exists.\n174|  (This is not unusual here — the repository has no application entry point at\n175|  all yet; `cmd/` does not exist.)\n176|- **F7** — the outcome now lists all 15 changed paths and they match the record.\n177|  See note N1 below for the one count that is still slightly generous.\n178|\n179|## G-F — battery and provenance\n180|\n181|Denominator re-derived independently rather than accepted. My own battery,\n182|reported with the classes split and the controls as distinct rows:\n183|\n184|| Class | Applied | Killed | Survived |\n185|| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n186|| narrowing | 13 | 13 | 0 |\n187|| arm-deletion | 6 | 6 | 0 |\n188|| census-only | 4 | 4 | 0 |\n189|| **total applied** | **23** | **23** | **0** |\n190|\n191|Distinct rows, NOT counted as applied mutants:\n192|\n193|| Control row | Count | Disposition |\n194|| --- | ---: | --- |\n195|| NOT_APPLIED (anchor absent) | 1 | RV18 — indentation drift; re-anchored as RV18b, then KILLED. Not a pass. |\n196|| COMPILE_FAIL | 2 | RV4 (unused `math`), RV12 (unused `observedMajor`) — re-anchored as RV5 and RV20, then KILLED. Not kills. |\n197|\n198|RV10 and RV11 are the same mutation measured twice (targeted `-run` mask, then\n199|the whole package) and are counted once.\n200|\n201|Additional kills beyond the gates above: RV6 generation match dropped; RV7\n202|member-set count dropped (killed by the *inventory bijection*, not only the\n203|parse test); RV8 version match narrowed to implementation-only; RV9 descriptor\n204|major gate dropped; RV20 failure-error contract major shifted off the observed\n205|major; RV22/RV23 ownership-registry 7.A claim self-minted / pointed at a\n206|non-existent declaration — both killed by `traceability`, so the registry\n207|cannot mint an ownership claim; RV24 instance-id UUIDv7 gate degraded to a\n208|length check; RV25 binding-digest match dropped; RV26 backend-id match dropped;\n209|RV27 generation match folds case.\n210|\n211|Provenance: `git status` shows exactly the 15 paths, unchanged. Leaf 1's own\n212|packages (`canonicaljson/*`, `environ/*`, `terminalbackend/manifest.go`) are\n213|untouched from `1d97474`. Of the four files this leaf shares with leaf 1,\n214|`identity.go` and `refusal_arm_inventory_test.go` are pure additions (0 deleted\n215|lines), and the `ownership.v0.5.0.json` deletions are confined to the §7.A\n216|`section_binding` row, which leaf 1 never touched. The referenced acceptance\n217|case `terminal-provider-descriptor-7a` exists in the registry.\n218|\n219|---\n220|\n221|## G1 (blocking) — the F2 fix reclassifies malformed versions as a recognized foreign major\n222|\n223|`parseMajor` saturates with an early **`return`** (`protocol.go:380`), which\n224|exits the function before the minor/patch validation at `protocol.go:386-395`\n225|ever runs. A version string whose first component overflows is therefore\n226|reported as *recognized* no matter what follows the first dot.\n227|\n228|Measured through the production entry point:\n229|\n230|| `protocol_version` in a frame | `parseMajor` | `DecodeResponse` |\n231|| --- | --- | --- |\n232|| `2.0.0` (control) | `(2, true)` | ADMITTED |\n233|| `3.0.0` | `(3, true)` | `incompatible_protocol`, exit 6 |\n234|| `18446744073709551618.0.0` | `(MaxInt, true)` | `incompatible_protocol`, exit 6 — correct, this is F2's fix working |\n235|| `2.abc.def` | `(0, false)` | `provider_protocol_error`, exit 13 |\n236|| **`99999999999999999999.abc.def`** | **`(MaxInt, true)`** | **`incompatible_protocol`, exit 6** |\n237|| **`99999999999999999999.0.x`** | **`(MaxInt, true)`** | **`incompatible_protocol`, exit 6** |\n238|| **`18446744073709551618..0`** (empty minor) | **`(MaxInt, true)`** | **`incompatible_protocol`, exit 6** |\n239|\n240|The three bold rows are not versions at all. Two shipped doc comments say they\n241|must not take this arm:\n242|\n243|- `protocol.go:359-360` — \"*parseMajor extracts the major from a strict numeric\n244|  X.Y.Z version. **Anything else is not a recognizable major, so the frame is\n245|  unusable rather than a mismatch.***\"\n246|- `protocol.go:403-404` — \"*A recognizable foreign major yields\n247|  incompatible_protocol; **every other unusable frame yields\n248|  provider_protocol_error**.*\"\n249|- and the new paragraph justifying the change asserts the narrower class it does\n250|  not enforce: \"*an **all-numeric** giant is observably foreign*\".\n251|  `99999999999999999999.abc.def` is not all-numeric.\n252|\n253|### Why it matters beyond the exit code\n254|\n255|The `foreignMajor` peek (`protocol.go:437-451`) arms on the same predicate, and\n256|arming it **skips the entire v2 member-vocabulary check** — the `ok` member read\n257|and `checkResponseMembers` both live inside `if foreignMajor == \"\"`\n258|(`protocol.go:454`). Measured on a frame carrying nothing but `protocol` and\n259|`protocol_version`:\n260|\n261|| bare frame version | refusal |\n262|| --- | --- |\n263|| `2.abc.def` | `provider_protocol_error` — `missing member` |\n264|| `99999999999999999999.abc.def` | `incompatible_protocol` — member checks never ran |\n265|\n266|That is a bypass path around a gate for a class of frames. It ends in a refusal\n267|today, so nothing is admitted — but the class that reaches the peek is now\n268|wider than the class the peek was written for, and a plugin chooses which\n269|refusal (and which exit status, 6 vs 13, i.e. version-negotiation vs\n270|malformed-frame) the host reports.\n271|\n272|### Nothing measures this\n273|\n274|I applied the candidate fix — replace the early `return math.MaxInt, true` with\n275|`major = math.MaxInt; continue`, so the loop still validates every digit and\n276|still falls through to the minor/patch checks — and:\n277|\n278|- all three bold rows return to `(0, false)` / `provider_protocol_error` exit 13;\n279|- `18446744073709551618.0.0` still saturates to `incompatible_protocol` exit 6,\n280|  so F2 stays closed;\n281|- **`go test ./internal/provhost/ -count=1` passes unchanged.**\n282|\n283|The whole suite is indifferent between the shipped behaviour and the corrected\n284|one. The shipped classification was never chosen — it fell out of the `return`.\n285|\n286|### Why the round-2 evidence did not catch it\n287|\n288|The outcome states, as the proof that nothing moved: \"*the provhost parse-arm\n289|census derives the identical set (saturation adds no `return 0, false`\n290|branch)*\". That is true and it is exactly the blind spot: `armParseBranches`\n291|enumerates **rejection branches**, so a change that moves inputs *between*\n292|existing branches is invisible to it. A branch census is not an effect census.\n293|This is a property inferred from a proxy signal and reported as fact — the same\n294|shape as rev1/F3, which is why `repeat-of` names it.\n295|\n296|### What the next round has to produce\n297|\n298|1. Make `parseMajor` validate the whole `X.Y.Z` shape before it reports a\n299|   recognized major (the `major = MaxInt; continue` form above is sufficient and\n300|   is already proven compatible with the existing suite).\n301|2. Add a named test row driving at least `<giant>.abc.def` and `<giant>..0`\n302|   through `DecodeResponse` and asserting `provider_protocol_error` / exit 13,\n303|   alongside the existing `TestParseMajorNeverWraps` rows asserting exit 6 for\n304|   the all-numeric giants — so the two classes are pinned apart and a future\n305|   short-circuit reddens.\n306|3. Correct the outcome's \"no gate was weakened\" sentence: state the arm-shift\n307|   that *was* intended (all-numeric giants: 13 → 6) as measured, and drop the\n308|   branch-census sentence as evidence for classification stability, or add the\n309|   effect measurement that would actually support it.\n310|\n311|---\n312|\n313|## Notes (not blocking)\n314|\n315|**N1 — the AC ratio is 14 driven + 1 stated bound, reported as \"15 of 15\n316|driven\".** Row 14 (F6, future caller recorded) says \"prose by nature\" in its own\n317|Driving-test cell. The DoD explicitly permits a stated bound in place of a\n318|driving test, so the row is *fine* — but counting it inside a \"15 of 15 driven\"\n319|ratio is the same generosity F7 flagged in round 1. Report it as\n320|`14 of 15 driven, 1 stated bound`.\n321|\n322|**N2 — the attestation tripwire matches call expressions only.** `f :=\n323|canonicaljson.VerifyObjectIdentity; f(x)` would not be seen, because only\n324|`CallExpr.Fun` is inspected. No such shape exists today (0 files reference the\n325|symbol outside its definition), and the scan is otherwise strong — AST-based,\n326|comment-immune, test-file-excluded, fails closed on `scanned == 0`, and\n327|control-planted. Worth one sentence as a stated bound on the test rather than a\n328|code change.\n329|\n330|**N3 — battery script provenance.** The outcome names `/tmp/mutA.py` and\n331|`/tmp/mutB.py` as the battery scripts. They happen to still exist, but `/tmp` is\n332|not a task-scoped artifact path; project convention is `.temp/<TASK-ID>/`. Not a\n333|correctness issue, only reproducibility.\n334|\n335|## What holds up (recorded so the next round does not re-litigate it)\n336|\n337|F1, F2, F4, F5 are closed as *classes*, not as sites; F3's bound reproduces on\n338|every sub-claim; the §7.A ownership move is coherent and the registry cannot\n339|mint it without the digest pin noticing; the descriptor match gate refuses on\n340|every one of its five dimensions under mutation; the lift's wire message carries\n341|no local secret and the causal-leak negative drives the real gate; the candidate\n342|tree is exactly the worktree and leaf 1 is unregressed. Only G1 needs work."}}}
